routes.brief:
First prerequisite: Arithmetic statistics, counting fields and Selmer distributions (ArithmeticStatistics), especially ST.0 (families and measures) and ST.5 (distributions, higher moments, function-field analogues); do not replan them. The new object is a probability theory of random profinite Γ-groups: Liu–Wood–Zureick-Brown, Invent. Math. 237 (2024), Part I (§§1–7), building on Liu–Wood, J. reine angew. Math. 762 (2020), which must be acquired and decomposed first because §§4–5 cite a dozen of its lemmas.

The final theorems, as in LWZB (arXiv v2 numbering):
- Theorem 1.2: X_{Γ,n} = 𝓕_n/[Y(S)] (n+1 Haar relations) converges weakly to a probability measure μ_Γ on admissible Γ-groups. The explicit formula (5.13) on basic opens holds only for H with Property E at level 𝒞; for other H the measure is 0 (sourceIssues E5). The printed formula has no such hypothesis and is wrong there.
- Theorem 5.12: μ_u is countably additive for every integer u (the printed u ≥ 0 is too weak for the later statements; sourceIssue E4).
- Theorem 5.15: the formula for μ_u(V_{𝒞,H}) for arbitrary 𝒞, again for H with Property E at level 𝒞 (E5).
- Theorem 6.2: E|Sur_Γ(X_u, A)| = [A : A^Γ]^{−u}.
- §7.1: abelianization gives Cohen–Lenstra–Martinet (use (Z_p[Γ]/(Σ g))^n, sourceIssue E3).
- Theorems 7.4–7.5: pro-p and p-class-c quotients give Boston–Bush–Hajir for Γ = Z/2.
- Conjecture 1.3, as a conjecture.

Cover also:
- admissibility, levels, pro-𝒞 completion and the space 𝒫 with basic opens;
- Property E (Definition 2.3);
- the free admissible group 𝓕_n (Lemma 3.1, Remark 3.2), the map Y and sections (Lemmas 3.4–3.6), Corollary 3.8 (for pro-|Γ|′ G, E12), Lemma 3.9, Corollary 3.10, Lemma 3.12, Proposition 3.13;
- Definition 3.15 (random groups and μ_{u,n}, μ_u);
- p-class tower groups in the model (Lemma 3.17, Proposition 3.22);
- the fundamental sequence (4.1)–(4.2) and multiplicities, Proposition 4.3, Lemma 4.6, Proposition 4.7, Corollary 4.8, Theorem 4.12 (reference corrected per E1);
- Γ-chief factor pairs (Definition 5.1, Lemma 5.2, Corollary 5.3), Lemma 5.4, Corollary 5.9, Lemma 5.10 (with the monotone-positivity hypothesis its proof needs, E10);
- profinite Schur–Zassenhaus including conjugacy of complements. Mathlib has only existence for finite groups; conjugacy in general needs Feit–Thompson (not in Mathlib or the atlas), so state it with an explicit solvability hypothesis or an imported Feit–Thompson, and track its consumers. Conjugacy is already used in Lemma 3.4, hence in Lemma 3.1, so all of Part I depends on it, as do Definition 2.1 and Lemma 9.3.

Import, never re-plan:
- free pro-p/pro-𝒞 groups on finite sets, Frattini theory and presentations: Profinite and pro-p groups (tauceti:TauCetiRoadmap/ProfiniteProPGroups), Layers 3–5;
- Haar measure, Carathéodory extension, Fatou and dominated convergence: Mathlib;
- finite group theory (chief series, Hall subgroups): Mathlib;
- the Galois-side facts (Proposition 2.2, Theorem 2.5): InverseGaloisAndArithmeticFundamentalGroups IG.4;
- class field theory for the abelianization: tauceti ClassFieldTheory.
Export μ_Γ and its moments to ArithmeticStatistics ST.5, whose function-field moment theorem (LWZB Theorem 1.4) is compared with Theorem 6.2.

Tests:
- Γ = Z/2 with H = Z/3 (Cohen–Lenstra real quadratic);
- Γ = Z/4 with H the Heisenberg group mod 3 (predicted moment 1/9, from Property E);
- Γ = Z/3 and the quaternion group example that fails Property E;
- a p-class tower group with d(H) = 2 for Γ = Z/2;
- Γ = Z/2, p = 3, 𝒞 the 3-Γ-groups of 3-class ≤ 2, H = F_3² with σ = −1: H lacks Property E, so the measure is 0. The printed (5.13) gives 2.70·10^{−3} (E5).
